{"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Article","modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}],"role":"article","title":"Localization"},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The default Empty\/Loading\/Error placeholders’ text — “Nothing Here,” “Loading,” “Retry,” “Something Went Wrong,” “Connection Issue” — comes from a String Catalog, ","type":"text"},{"code":"Resources\/Localizable.xcstrings","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", resolved from ScreenStates’ own bundle (","type":"text"},{"code":"Bundle.module","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") rather than your app’s. That’s deliberate: a custom title your app passes in (see below) is never run back through localization, and a language you add to ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"your own","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" app’s catalog has no effect on these five strings — they only ever resolve against ScreenStates’ bundle. English, Spanish, French, and Russian are built in today.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"Adding-a-language-to-ScreenStates","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Adding a language to ScreenStates"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"This is a change to the package itself, not something a consuming app can do on its own — it needs a PR. Open ","type":"text"},{"code":"Resources\/Localizable.xcstrings","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and add a ","type":"text"},{"code":"localizations","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" entry for the new language code under each of the five string keys:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"codeListing","code":["\"Retry\" : {","  \"comment\" : \"Title of the button that retries loading after an error.\",","  \"localizations\" : {","    \"en\" : { \"stringUnit\" : { \"state\" : \"translated\", \"value\" : \"Retry\" } },","    \"de\" : { \"stringUnit\" : { \"state\" : \"translated\", \"value\" : \"Wiederholen\" } }","  }","}"],"syntax":"json"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Every one of the five keys needs an entry for every language — a string present in English but missing a translation elsewhere fails CI (see below). ","type":"text"},{"code":"ScreenStatesStrings.swift","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"‘s doc comments list all five keys and their English text if you’re starting from scratch.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"Overriding-text-per-app","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overriding text per-app"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"You don’t need a new language, or a PR, to show different text than the built-in default — two options, depending on how much you want to replace:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView(title:)"},{"type":"text","text":" takes a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"title"},{"type":"text","text":" directly, bypassing "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Localizable.xcstrings"},{"type":"text","text":" entirely for that one string. Pass your own localized string (from your own app’s String Catalog, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"NSLocalizedString"},{"type":"text","text":", whatever) instead of the default:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","code":["ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView(title: String(localized: \"No Results\"))"],"type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultErrorView"},{"type":"text","text":" has no equivalent for its title or Retry button text today — only the error’s own message is customizable, via "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDisplayableError"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"For anything else — the Error placeholder’s title, the Retry button’s text, or any text at all in a shape the built-in views don’t expose a parameter for — pass a fully custom placeholder instead of the default one, via "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateView"},{"type":"text","text":"’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"empty"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"loading"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"error"},{"type":"text","text":" builder closures (see "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit"},{"type":"text","text":") or "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateViewController"},{"type":"text","text":"‘s equivalent. A custom placeholder can still reuse "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState\/accessibilityAnnouncement"},{"type":"text","text":" so VoiceOver announces transitions with the same wording your custom text uses, instead of the default’s."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}]}]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/localization"]}],"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Localization","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates"]]},"sections":[],"kind":"article","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"How the default placeholders’ built-in text is localized, how to add a language, and how to show different text instead without touching ScreenStates itself."}],"seeAlsoSections":[{"anchor":"Related-Documentation","identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Accessibility"],"title":"Related Documentation"}],"references":{"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenStateDisplayableError":{"type":"topic","title":"ScreenStateDisplayableError","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"ScreenStateDisplayableError","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/screenstatedisplayableerror","fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"ScreenStateDisplayableError","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"text":"Lets an error type supply its own user-facing message, distinct from","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"localizedDescription","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — which is often a debugging string (or, for a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"plain ","type":"text"},{"code":"Error","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" with no ","type":"text"},{"code":"CustomStringConvertible","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"LocalizedError"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"conformance, just the type name) rather than something to put in front"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"of the person using the app."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDisplayableError","kind":"symbol","role":"symbol"},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/Accessibility":{"type":"topic","title":"Accessibility","url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/accessibility","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Accessibility","kind":"article","abstract":[{"text":"Everything the default placeholders — and your own custom ones — get for free: VoiceOver, Reduce Motion, Increase Contrast, Dynamic Type, and sta